Manual physiological in silico digestion model (DigSim)
George van Aken

Introduction 

In this folder you will find a map that contains an executable of the physiological in silico digestion program DigSim with two Excel help workbooks plus a short manual. 


Manual 

The program is written in Pascal and consists of a main program DigSim{version} Pascal source code and various units (also as Pascal source code).exe) that are used by the main program. To use it you download the main repository onto the local disk on your computer, into a directory under username/git/ . To run the program you need to download and install Lazarus (freeware Pascal) on your computer.  Clicking on the main (DigSim(version), Lazarus opens the main program and the subunits. By clicking "run" the program is compied to an executable that is stored into the directory with extension .exe and executed. The program searches for input form meal.txt, which defines the diet as a timing of a sequence of various meals, in the same directory as DigSim, and writes output in Results.txt in that same directory.  

You can make changes in meal.txt, but it is much more convenient to use the excel workbook template for it (a few example cases are attached). You can change the meal in these workbooks and save the first tab as a tab-delimited text file under the name meal.txt. In my definition, a meal consists of various compounds (sugar, starch, water, protein, tracer compounds, etc.), has a typical particle radius, is a liquid (solid = 0) or consists of solid particles each with the same radius (“solid” in mm) and if liquid, has a viscosity (mPas)) introduced at one of each five plates a given timing.    

The meal menu starts with fields to define the menu (first sheet of the workbook, the other sheets are not really used, but can be linked to the first sheet if convenient). A menu consists of amounts (in grams) of one or more compounds (defined by compound numbers) added to one or more dishes (currently 1-5) at given times (minutes) from which the compounds are consumed at different defined rates. The purpose of the various dishes is that the compounds are not necessarily eaten together in one bite, but often in sequence and at different rates (e.g. a drink from a cup (e.g. dish 1) and a solid meal on a separate dish (e.g. dish 2)). The format is strict, the headers must remain in place, rows must remain continuous (no empty lines) and the menu must end with 9999.  The number of compounds and their timing are however unlimited. 

 

Below the menu field the properties and structural behavior of the compounds can be defined. It starts with the properties of the compounds, which are free to define within the following fields. New compounds can be added to the first row, but the row must end by 9999:

 
In addition to parameters such as compound pH, particle size (for liquid set to 0) and liquid viscosity, the fields allow to include structural and compositional disintegration of the compounds. The way I do it now for general purposes is to define an first order (exponential) decay into two other compounds, for example the decomposition time of solid 1-mm pieces of chocolate in the mouth is set to 5 second in which it melts to give molten chocolate (Chocolate in stomach composition) and releases some sucrose (5% of weight). in the stomach any still remaining chocolate has similar melting time and decomposition. The molten chocolate is a high viscosity liquid, which decompose in the mouth and stomach in its components (given by compound “0”) which are fat and sugar. This approach is handy to see the effect of delayed decomposition of e.g. solids in the stomach and the half-time can be estimated from e.g. in vitro studies, but of course amore dedicated modelling of for example casein gelation and bolus erosion and the effect of pH and enzymes in this can also be included in the program itself. The particle radius of the solid compounds are used to estimate creaming or sedimentation in the stomach by Stokes law (together with the densities and viscosity of the medium) and the retention of solid particles by the Pylorus.    

The last set of fields are used to define the ingredient composition (in mass %; 80 in number) of the compounds. These 80 ingredients are defined with the DigSim program and cannot be changed, only the % of each ingredient in the compounds can be altered: 

 

Changes made in the Meal workbook can be saved under any name. To make it available for the DigSim program, the first sheet of the workbook should be saved as a tab-delimited text file “meal.txt” in the same directory as DigSim. 

The internal parameters are now set to an average individual. It will open with a screen asking you the number of calculation steps per minute, the larger more accurate and slower. For a quick reasonable assessment I chose 16 or 18, for an accurate calculation for example 60 or 63. After entering this number, the program starts with an equilibration for 500 min up to the fasted state (8 stars, each star corresponds to the completion of 1 hour simulated time) and will then show the subsequent additions of meals (and simulated hours by stars). After the last meal the simulation is continued for 5 hours (eight stars) after which the program stores data for each full minute. Because the simulated stomach in the program has a peristaltic cycle of 3 times per minute, it matters if the chosen step size is a multitude of 3 or not (if not, you will see the variations related to the different stages in the gastric peristalsis). Digsim automatically stores the output in the same directory as the Digsim program under Results.txt. 

You can extract and plot the data from the result.txt file as you wish, the data are currently stored as tab-delimited. For easy extraction and visualization I added the “Simulated” workbooks, which shows a lot of graphs related to the data in Result.txt, and the data can be updated/refreshed by the option in the Excel menu bar. Of course you can save a new “Simulated” workbook under any desired name.   

